Q: XmlSerializer - There was an error reflecting type A: <p>In my case sgen.exe was having problem with two classes that had the same name. But these classes were generated by a code generator, and it would have been difficult and ugly to change the names of the classes. But in any case I didn't even need these classes to be serializable. I found a better solution was to specify which types I wanted sgen.exe to generate code for.</p>
<p>I found the following config code at this blog post, which explains how to do it: <a href="https://mikhail.io/2014/05/21/sgen-to-precompile-classes-for-xmpserializer/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mikhail Shilkov: Sgen to precompile classes for XmlSerializer</a> .</p>
<p>The types to generate code for are specified in the <code>&lt;SgenTypes ... /&gt;</code> elements.</p>
<p>It worked for me. An added benefit is that it reduces bloat in the generated assemblies.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Target Name=&quot;AfterBuild&quot; DependsOnTargets=&quot;AssignTargetPaths;Compile;ResolveKeySource&quot; Inputs=&quot;$(MSBuildAllProjects);@(IntermediateAssembly)&quot; Outputs=&quot;$(OutputPath)$(_SGenDllName)&quot;&gt;
    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
      &lt;SgenTypes Include=&quot;MyNamespace.MySerializableClass1&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;SgenTypes Include=&quot;MyNamespace.MySerializableClass2&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;SgenTypes Include=&quot;MyNamespace.MySerializableClassN&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;Delete Files=&quot;$(TargetDir)$(TargetName).XmlSerializers.dll&quot; ContinueOnError=&quot;true&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;SGen BuildAssemblyName=&quot;$(TargetFileName)&quot; BuildAssemblyPath=&quot;$(OutputPath)&quot; References=&quot;@(ReferencePath)&quot; ShouldGenerateSerializer=&quot;true&quot; UseProxyTypes=&quot;false&quot; KeyContainer=&quot;$(KeyContainerName)&quot; KeyFile=&quot;$(KeyOriginatorFile)&quot; DelaySign=&quot;$(DelaySign)&quot; ToolPath=&quot;$(SGenToolPath)&quot; Types=&quot;@(SgenTypes)&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Output TaskParameter=&quot;SerializationAssembly&quot; ItemName=&quot;SerializationAssembly&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/SGen&gt;
&lt;/Target&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I use async function as Promise executor in js? A: <pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {I_am_async_function().then(resolve).catch(reject)});

</code></pre>
<p>UPDATE</p>
<p>You can replace the resolve and reject in then and catch with custom function that call resolve or reject.</p>
<p>In my use case:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import fs from &quot;node:fs/promises&quot;;
import { pipeline } from &quot;node:stream&quot;;
await new Promise(r =&gt; fs.open(absolutePath).then(handle =&gt; pipeline(handle.createReadStream(), I_am_write_stream, r)));
</code></pre>
<p>UPDATE</p>
<p>The example above was a bad one, as it doesn't handle errors correctly. Correct example by Estus Flask:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await new Promise((r, rej) =&gt; pipeline(handle.createReadStream(), I_am_write_stream, (err, val) =&gt; err ? rej(err) : r(val)));
</code></pre>
